    ('0e368bbc-87e1-42e4-b521-62b036c9be03'::uuid, 'mystery', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:0e368bbc-87e1-42e4-b521-62b036c9be03:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''4'' restored on Castell Caer Seion', '50443e66ac5ddb94ae3a90337a4521b5', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 1 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 235 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81820", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2044:chunk-0001: description ''Castell Caer Seion is an Iron Age hillfort on the summit of Conwy Mountain in North Wales. Unusually, the main fort contains a smaller, more heavily defended inner fort with its own defences and entrance. Radiocarbon dating places the earliest occupation between the 6th and 4th centuries BC, with the inner fort built around the 4th century BC. Over 400 sling stones and remains of approximately 50 hut circles were found within the main walls. The diet of inhabitants included wheat, oat, spelt, barley, and hazelnut. The fort was likely abandoned when the Romans conquered North Wales in 78 AD.'' -> ''Castell Caer Seion is an Iron Age hillfort situated at the top of Conwy Mountain, in Conwy County [1]. It is unusual for the fact that the main fort contains a smaller, more heavily defended fort, complete with its own distinct defences and entrance, with no obvious means of access between the two [1]. The construction date of the original fort is still unknown, but recent excavations have revealed evidence of occupation as early as the 6th century BC, whilst the smaller fort can be dated with reasonable certainty to around the 4th century BC [1]. Whilst the forts were constructed in different periods, archaeologists have uncovered evidence of concurrent occupation, seemingly up until around the 2nd century BC [1]. The larger fort contained around 50 roundhouses during its lifetime, whereas examinations of the smaller fort have turned up no more than six [1]. The fort is on the summit of a ridge of rhyolite at an altitude of approximately 800 feet and is enclosed by a single rampart [1]. The site has a commanding position overlooking Conwy Bay [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93304", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93305", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:234573", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:234574", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-600''"}]'::jsonb),
