    ('133ef307-1b56-4af3-9411-cd66947d8cc0'::uuid, 'antiquity', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:133ef307-1b56-4af3-9411-cd66947d8cc0:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: antiquity ''5'' restored on Berth Hill', '4d61112e3d17aae6add75be47728aaa2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 6 from period_start -1500"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81940", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2047:chunk-0001: description ''Berth Hill is an Iron Age multivallate hillfort near the village of Maer in Staffordshire, about 8 km south-west of Newcastle-under-Lyme, and a Scheduled Monument. Sited on a sandstone outcrop at the south-east edge of Maer Hills, the irregular defences measure 320 m by 150 m, enclosing roughly 3.75 hectares. The earth-and-stone rampart is up to 15 m wide; on the less steep northern slope a second outer rampart was added. Two original entrances survive: an inturned entrance in the south-west and another at the north end of the east defences. Large multivallate hillforts of this type are nationally rare, with only about 50 examples recorded across England.'' -> ''Berth Hill is an Iron Age hillfort in Staffordshire, England, about 5 miles south-west of Newcastle-under-Lyme, and near the village of Maer [1]. The fort is on a sandstone outcrop at the south-east edge of Maer Hills [1]. The defences, following the contours of the hill, have an irregular shape [1]. Their length is 320 metres north-west to south-east, and the width is 150 metres, enclosing an area of about 3.75 hectares [1]. The rampart, of earth and stone, is up to 15 metres wide and 0.8 metres above the interior [1]. In the north, the slope is less steep than elsewhere, and there is a second rampart outside this [1]. There are two original entrances: an inturned entrance in the south-west, and an entrance at the north end of the east defences [1]. Results of excavations in 1966 and 1975 have been interpreted as showing an early period of occupation, followed by a long interval of disuse (suggested by ditches containing silt and parts of collapsed rampart), and later a hurried reconstruction of the defences [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93396", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93397", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246089", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246090", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384976", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384977", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:399555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:399556", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start None -> ''-1500''"}]'::jsonb),
