    ('158bcd5a-1203-4ddf-a640-d86ee5c47e66'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:158bcd5a-1203-4ddf-a640-d86ee5c47e66:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Guntupalli Buddhist Caves', '3be36a17068320dedfe00bb6e4c1d876', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Temple complex'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 53 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81976", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2048:chunk-0001: description ''A group of Buddhist monuments near Kamavarapukota, Eluru district, Andhra Pradesh, dating from the 3rd-2nd century BCE to the 5th-6th century CE. Known as \"Andhra Ajanta,\" the site predates the famous Ajanta and Ellora caves. It features rock-cut caves including the Vritta Chaitya with a vaulted domical ceiling, brick viharas, a chaitya hall, and over 60 hilltop votive stupas. The complex is associated with Hinayana Buddhism and the great Buddhist logician Dignaga. Protected by the Archaeological Survey of India.'' -> ''The Guntupalle or Guntupalli Group of Buddhist Monuments is located near Kamavarapukota, Eluru district, in the state of Andhra Pradesh in India [1]. The rock-cut part of the site has two Buddhist caves, a chaitya hall and a large group of stupas [1]. The chaitya hall has a rare carved stone entrance replicating wooden architecture, a simpler version of that at the Lomas Rishi Cave [1]. There are remains of structural buildings in brick and stone, including remains of two vihara made of brick, as well as excavated caves at two levels, including an unusual structural chaitya hall (that is, one built above ground) [1]. The core of this consists of the stone stupa with an enclosed path around it allowing ritual parikrama (circumambulation) [1]. They mostly date to 200-0 BCE, with some sculptures added later [1]. The main building above ground is in brick, around a stone stupa, with over 30 smaller stupas on a terrace in front of it [1]. During excavation, three relic caskets were found [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:143427", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/158bcd5a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/158bcd5a/002 View from the Maha Stupa.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:307635", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/158bcd5a/002 View from the Maha Stupa.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/158bcd5a/Entrance_of_Rock-cut_temple.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
