 The remains as seen today are mostly of Iron Age origin, but further earthworks, probably of medieval origin, lie on the south-eastern slopes [1]. The remains of the two walls of stone can be seen, as can those of a chevaux-de-frise [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94385", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94386", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:143926", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-004: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Pen-y-gaer_from_the_north.jpg/400px-Pen-y-gaer_from_the_north.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/19ec6c34/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246473", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s003: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246474", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s003: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385004", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385005", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:399583", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:399584", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start None -> ''-3000''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('19ed3a0f-09e5-4844-ae5d-9d50bea1371b'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:19ed3a0f-09e5-4844-ae5d-9d50bea1371b:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''7'' restored on Grottos of Juxtlahuaca', 'b4ac9d20985b9772680f75c6c164e9b7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cave Structures'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 12 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82122", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2052:chunk-0001: description ''Juxtlahuaca is a cave system in Guerrero, Mexico, containing some of the earliest sophisticated painted art in Mesoamerica, dated to the Early Preclassic period (ca. 1200-900 BC). The Olmec-style murals, located over a kilometer deep inside the cave, include a 2-meter-tall scene of human dominance, a red Feathered Serpent with green plumes, and a red jaguar. First professionally documented in the 1960s by Gillett Griffin and Carlo Gay, the cave also contains the Hall of the Dead with a dozen ancient skeletons covered in stalactite crust. It is the only known example of non-Maya deep cave art in Mesoamerica.'' -> ''Juxtlahuaca, or Xiuxtlahuaca, is a cave and archaeological site in the Mexican state of Guerrero containing murals linked to the Olmec motifs and iconography [1]. Along with the nearby Oxtotitlán cave, Juxtlahuaca walls contain the earliest sophisticated painted art known in Mesoamerica, and only known example of non-Maya deep cave art in Mesoamerica [1]. The Juxtlahuaca site is located some 45 km southeast of the state capital Chilpancingo in what is now a National Park [1]. The entire cave system is slightly over 5 km [1]. The most well-known of the cave art is Painting 1, which features a large bearded man with a black cloak, a striped tunic, and an elaborate headpiece [1]. Also of note in Juxtlahuaca is a painting of a red Feathered Serpent with green plumes, near a red jaguar whose large ears and eyes give it a youthful cast [1]. A dozen skeletons have been found in the so-called Hall of the Dead [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:307722", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/19ed3a0f/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/19ed3a0f/Juxtlahuaca_Ruler_(M_Lachniet).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384177", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4400:chunk-0001: description ''Juxtlahuaca, or Xiuxtlahuaca, is a cave and archaeological site in the Mexican state of Guerrero containing murals linked to the Olmec motifs and iconography [1]. Along with the nearby Oxtotitlán cave, Juxtlahuaca walls contain the earliest sophisticated painted art known in Mesoamerica, and only known example of non-Maya deep cave art in Mesoamerica [1]. The Juxtlahuaca site is located some 45 km southeast of the state capital Chilpancingo in what is now a National Park [1]. The entire cave system is slightly over 5 km [1]. The most well-known of the cave art is Painting 1, which features a large bearded man with a black cloak, a striped tunic, and an elaborate headpiece [1]. Also of note in Juxtlahuaca is a painting of a red Feathered Serpent with green plumes, near a red jaguar whose large ears and eyes give it a youthful cast [1]. A dozen skeletons have been found in the so-called Hall of the Dead [1].'' -> ''The most well-known of the cave art is Painting 1, which features a large bearded man with a black cloak, a striped tunic, and an elaborate headpiece [1] [2].''"}]'::jsonb),
