t None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338475", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4325:chunk-0001: description None -> \"Hadsar Fort is a stone hill fort standing in ruins in the Junnar region of Pune district, Maharashtra [1]. The fort is believed to have been built in the Satavahana era, and its entrance gate, sentries'' rooms, water cisterns and steps are all hewn out of one rock [1]. The Treaty of Purandar between King Shivaji and the Moghuls named the fort, and British troops took the stronghold in 1818 [1]. A temple of Mahdev stands at the top of the fort and holds stone-cut idols of Ganesh, Garud and Hanuman [1]. The fort stands near Hadsar village, which lies 13 km north-west of Junnar [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346398", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346399", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
