polis when the fortification was extended during the 13th century BC [1]. The site circle has a diameter of 27.5 m and contains six shaft graves [1]. Among the funerary gifts found were a series of gold death masks, full sets of weapons, ornate staffs, gold jewelry, as well as gold and silver cups [1]. The site was excavated by the archaeologist Heinrich Schliemann and Panagiotis Stamatakis in 1876–77, following the descriptions of Homer and Pausanias [1]. A total of nineteen bodies – eight men, nine women, and two children – were found in the shafts [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144008", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/1c4dc9a8/hero.webp'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/1c4dc9a8/07.Ταφικός κύκλος A'' GR-J11-0049.webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333683", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-001: thumbnail_url \"/data/images/wiki/1c4dc9a8/07.Ταφικός κύκλος A'' GR-J11-0049.webp\" -> ''/data/images/wiki/1c4dc9a8/Grave-Circle-A-Mycenae.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
