ry, and digital humanities to study the region''s cultural landscape from the Bronze Age through the Classical period.\" -> \"Desfina is a town in southern Phocis, Greece [1]. The area has far older significance: on the hill of Kastrouli, a fortified Mycenaean settlement from the 13th-12th century BC features Cyclopean walls approximately 1.2-1.4 m wide, tentatively identified with Homeric Anemoreia [1] [2] [3]. The Southern Phokis Regional Project now conducts transdisciplinary research combining classics, archaeochemistry, and digital humanities to study the region''s cultural landscape [3].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246531", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s003: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246532", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s003: period_start None -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347173", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347174", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1f66729b-c7e8-476a-b9cc-84b23a36074f'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:1f66729b-c7e8-476a-b9cc-84b23a36074f:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''7'' restored on Porth Hellick Down', '0cf6c266c570a8b58b0c1096dd09fea4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cemetery'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:73842", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0901:chunk-0001: description ''Porth Hellick Down is a Neolithic and Bronze Age burial site on St Mary\\''s, Isles of Scilly, featuring at least six entrance graves -- the largest surviving assembly of this rare tomb type in Britain [1]. The best-preserved chamber, known as \"The Great Tomb,\" dates to c. 1500 BC and features a near-circular mound 39 feet in diameter with a stone-lined burial chamber roofed by four massive capstones [2]. Excavated by George Bonsor in 1899, the site also includes unchambered cairns and a prehistoric field system with a linear boundary of small upright stones [1][2].'' -> ''Porth Hellick Down is a Neolithic and Bronze Age archeological site located on the island of St Mary\\''s, in the Isles of Scilly in Great Britain [1]. The ancient burial monument encompasses a large cairn cemetery that includes at least six entrance graves, other unchambered cairns, and a prehistoric field system [1]. The largest and best preserved entrance grave on the ancient site is a Bronze Age grave (2000-1500 BC) located on the northwestern slope of Porth Hellick Down [1]. The burial monument is situated close to the boundary of a prehistoric field system [1]. The site was excavated by the antiquarian, George Bonsor in 1899 [1]. He named the entrance grave, \"The Great Tomb\" [1]. Earlier unrecorded excavations at the site had left the burial chamber empty, except for a piece of pumice and a few remnants of late Bronze Age pottery fragments [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:94515", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s003: site_type ''Cairn'' -> ''Cemetery''"}]'::jsonb),
