 to the River Severn under clear conditions [1][3]. Since 1971, Swindon Borough Council has managed the 42-hectare site as a country park, preserving its archaeological and landscape value [1][3].'' -> ''Barbury Castle is an Iron Age hillfort in Wiltshire, England [1] [2]. Located about 5.5 miles south of Swindon on Barbury Hill, it sits along the ancient Ridgeway route—a prehistoric pathway crossing the chalk downs of southern England [1] [3]. The fortification features two deep defensive ditches and ramparts [1]. The site lies within the Wessex Downs Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty and commands views across the Cotswolds to the River Severn under clear conditions [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235611", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235612", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''-700''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308510", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/230e6523/Barbury Castle Aerial View (2).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/230e6523/Barbury_Hill_Iron_Age_Fort.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
