ing tree. Similar rock signs are common across Southern Azerbaijan and Eastern Anatolia.'' -> ''The Nakhchivan Rock Signs were discovered in 2016 at Agsal, between the villages of Erefse and Milax in the Culfa (Julfa) region of Azerbaijan [1] [2]. Three rock signs were found featuring drainage channels directed towards the canyon, suggesting fluid flow [1]. Similar rock signs are common across Southern Azerbaijan and Eastern Anatolia [1] [3].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246885", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246886", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-10000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347192", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347193", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''-10000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
