nd 25–20 BC [1]. Besides being one of the most important Roman-period ruins in the city, it is also known for the Monumentum Ancyranum [1]. This is an inscription about the works of Augustus [1]. It is the most complete copy of Res Gestae Divi Augusti that has survived to the present day [1]. An earlier, 2nd century BCE Phrygian temple on the site was destroyed [1]. Only the side walls and the ornamented door frame remain; the positions of six columns can still be recognized [1]. After the death of Augustus in AD 14, a copy of the text of the Res Gestae Divi Augusti was inscribed on both walls inside the pronaos in Latin with a Greek translation on an exterior wall of the cella [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144687", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-005: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/AugustusAnkara2.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/24009e21/Monumento de Ancira, Ankara, Turquía, 2024-10-03, DD 48.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308524", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/24009e21/Monumento de Ancira, Ankara, Turquía, 2024-10-03, DD 48.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/24009e21/AugustusAnkara2.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('24018f0c-73bc-4af8-bf08-bd05b74c2c9f'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:24018f0c-73bc-4af8-bf08-bd05b74c2c9f:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''7'' restored on Kerameikos', 'eb766c84347102ad343ed560e381aca6', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cemetery'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:91852", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2211:chunk-0001: description \"Kerameikos (5th–6th c. BC) was the ancient potters'' quarter of Athens, located northwest of the Acropolis [1]. Its name derives from keramos (pottery), giving English ''ceramic'' [1]. The area encompassed both intra- and extramural sections, straddling the Dipylon Gate along the Eridanos River [1]. It housed the city''s principal cemetery, where lavish funerary monuments and sculptures lined the Sacred Way to Eleusis [1].\" -> ''Kerameikos, also known by its Latinized form Ceramicus, is an area of Athens, Greece, located to the northwest of the Acropolis, which includes an extensive area both within and outside the ancient city walls, on both sides of the Dipylon Gate and by the banks of the Eridanos River [1]. Archaeological excavations in the Kerameikos began in 1870 under the auspices of the Greek Archaeological Society [1]. Latest finds in the Kerameikos include the excavation of a 2.1 m tall Kouros [1]. During the construction of Kerameikos metro station for the expanded Athens Metro, a plague pit and approximately 1,000 tombs from the 4th and 5th centuries BC were discovered [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95265", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95266", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-2700''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144688", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/24018f0c/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/24018f0c/Kerameikos Cemetery from Melidone Street on June 19, 2020.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308527", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/24018f0c/Kerameikos Cemetery from Melidone Street on June 19, 2020.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/24018f0c/Kerameikos_Cemetery_on_July_28,_2019.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
