f places in England to be named after the pre-Christian deity Woden, the leader of the Old English pantheon [1]. During the Anglo-Saxon period there are believed to have been two battles fought in Wednesbury, in 592 and 715 [1]. According to The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle there was \"a great slaughter\" in 592 and \"Ceawlin was driven out\" [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95307", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95308", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144726", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-005: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/St_Bartholomew%27s_Wednesbury.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/264ac74e/Wednesbury_-_the_High_Street.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246919", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246920", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308570", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/264ac74e/Wednesbury_-_the_High_Street.webp'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/264ac74e/St_Bartholomew''s_Wednesbury.webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347214", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347215", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('26c567ca-9af0-486b-9599-dcb0b7792628'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:26c567ca-9af0-486b-9599-dcb0b7792628:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''6'' restored on Anakeion', '37fcb311e4964aff2c7f64b3d0fac048', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Temple complex'', ''Undated'') is shared by 7 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82382", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2060:chunk-0001: description ''A 5th-century BC sanctuary in Athens dedicated to the Dioskouroi (Castor and Pollux), located on the east slope of the Acropolis near the cave of Aglauros. It served as a key military assembly point, as described by Thucydides when soldiers gathered there during the oligarchic unrest of 411 BC. The annual Anakeia festival held here featured animal sacrifices for military protection. Its exact location remains debated, with limited archaeological remains identified.'' -> ''The Anakeion or Anacaeum, also known as the Sanctuary of the Dioskouroi, was a temple in Athens [1]. The name of the temple derives from the Greek Ἄνακες (an archaic form of ἄνακτες, \"lords\" or \"kings\"), the title by which the Dioskouroi, Castor and Pollux, twin sons of Zeus and Leda, were commonly known in Attica [1]. The Old Agora, the predecessor of the Classical Agora, was used in the fifth century and before as a rallying point, and references in Andokides and Thucydides to musters at the Anakeion in 415 and 411 BCE have led scholars to conclude that the Anakeion may have lain within the vicinity of the Old Agora, perhaps to the east of the Acropolis [1]. The decoration of the Anakeion, according to Pausanias, fell to Mikon and Polygnotos [1]. The former depicted the Argonauts, the followers of Jason, with particular attention to Akastos and his horses [1]. Polygnotos depicted the Rape of the Leukippides: the forcible abduction and marriage of Phoebe and Hilaeira, daughters of Leukippos, by the Dioskouroi [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95309", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95310", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144744", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/26c567ca/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246921", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246922", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''43''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347219", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347220", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''43'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
