    ('277970ce-65f8-403a-8e20-dbcadac0f214'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:277970ce-65f8-403a-8e20-dbcadac0f214:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Eston Nab', '50eb64497f0d97a6a0e4f86d08aa5447', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fort'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 62 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33457", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0019:chunk-0001: description ''Eston Nab is a rocky promontory at 242 metres elevation in Redcar and Cleveland, England, overlooking the Tees Valley. The site shows evidence of human activity from the Mesolithic period (c. 8500 BC) through flint finds, with Neolithic flintwork also recovered. Excavations in 1927-29, 1966-68, and 1985-87 revealed Bronze Age barrows with cremation urns and burnt bone, followed by two successive Late Bronze Age palisaded enclosures. The univallate hillfort proper, measuring 250 x 120 metres, features a ditch and rampart with boulder wall constructed in the early Iron Age. A 2017 Heritage Lottery excavation discovered what researchers believe is an ancient henge at the site.'' -> ''A nab is a rocky promontory, and Eston Nab, marking the highest point – at 242 metres – on the escarpment which forms Eston Hills, appears as a clear sandstone cliff on the northernmost edge of Eston Moor [1]. It overlooks the town of Eston, which is part of Redcar and Cleveland [1]. It is the site of Bronze Age burial mounds and an Iron Age hill fort [1]. Frank Elgee, curator of the Dorman Museum, Middlesbrough, in 1927, uncovered parts of an earthenware cremation urn, together with burnt bone and flint [1]. These possibly dated from 1800 BC [1]. There was a substantial Iron Age hill fort at Eston Nab [1]. Boulder walls and ditches are still visible even though they were built in around 700 BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114859", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114860", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: period_start ''-6000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:114861", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s002: site_type ''Mound/tumulus'' -> ''Fort''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145350", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/277970ce/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/277970ce/Eston Nab from Flatts Lane.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235639", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235640", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''-700''"}]'::jsonb),
