    ('29074533-09e3-4f7b-9d43-dbffbf8a0a32'::uuid, 'mystery', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:29074533-09e3-4f7b-9d43-dbffbf8a0a32:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''4'' restored on Hawkesdown Hill', '7ae61c168cc04448d14f751c2950f9c3', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 1 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 235 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82462", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2062:chunk-0001: description ''Hawkesdown Hill, also known as Hawkesdown Camp, is an Iron Age hill fort situated on a prominent hillside above the Axe Estuary near Axmouth in Devon, England[1]. The fort stands approximately 130 metres (430 ft) above sea level and dates to between 1500 and 500 BC[1]. As a hill fort, it served as a defended settlement and administrative center for the local Iron Age community[1]. The site occupies a strategic position overlooking the estuary, providing both natural defense and access to maritime routes along the coast[1].'' -> ''Hawkesdown Hill, also known as Hawkesdown Camp, is an Iron Age Hill fort close to Axmouth in Devon [1]. It is situated on a prominent hillside above the Axe Estuary and is approximately 130 metres above sea level [1]. The hill fort has a single earthwork boundary enclosing about 2.5 hectares with an outwork to the east [1]. The roughly rectangular interior is some 250 metres in length, on an east-west orientation, with a width of some 100 metres [1]. The boundary consists of a rampart, which survives in places to a height of 4 metres, surrounded by a U-shaped ditch over 2 metres deep [1]. The outwork lies 100 metres to the east and is 17.8 metres wide and 0.4 metres high [1]. Remains found on the site suggest it was subject to an attack by the Romans, although the circumstances are unrecorded, and may simply reflect use of an abandoned site for training purposes [1]. Hawkesdown Camp was designated as a scheduled monument in 1924 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95365", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95366", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302104", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302105", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_start None -> ''-800''"}]'::jsonb),
