e site at its peak covered approximately 17 acres and featured at least three sunken temples, multiple platforms, and a central plaza. Excavations between 2005-2007 uncovered 972 bone fragments from at least 25 individuals in a circular structure, revealing evidence of a mortuary ritual where specialists used heated caustic limewater to disarticulate and clean bones of the deceased before returning them to families.'' -> ''Qhunqhu Wankani (also known as Khonkho Wankane) is a ceremonial centre in Bolivia predating the rise of the nearby city of Tiwanaku [1] [2] [3]. Located south of Lake Titicaca in Ingavi Province, the site at its peak covered approximately 17 acres and featured at least three sunken temples, multiple platforms, and a central plaza [1] [3] [4]. Excavations between 2005-2007 uncovered 972 bone fragments from at least 25 individuals in a circular structure [3] [5].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235668", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: site_type None -> ''Temple complex''"}]'::jsonb),
