]. The site remains on the Via del Tempio di Iside.'' -> ''The Temple of Isis is a Roman temple in Pompeii dedicated to the Egyptian goddess Isis [1] [2]. This small and almost intact temple was one of the first discoveries during the excavation of Pompeii in 1764 [1] [2]. Its role as a Hellenized Egyptian temple in a Roman colony was confirmed with an inscription documented by Francisco la Vega on July 20, 1765 [1] [3]. Original paintings and sculptures from the temple are now displayed at the Museo Archeologico in Naples [1] [2]. The site remains on the Via del Tempio di Iside [1] [4].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236610", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:236611", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s003: period_start None -> ''-200''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:309504", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/383214cc/Fresco at Temple of Isis, Pompeii (48754547893).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/383214cc/4_Pompei0030.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
