    ('3d296ec1-adcb-43da-ae08-ffcdb378d4be'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:3d296ec1-adcb-43da-ae08-ffcdb378d4be:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''6'' restored on Devil''s Arrows', '58eaa97c28aa91e789ae8bbb622b4e12', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 28 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43347", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0073:chunk-0001: description \"The Devil''s Arrows are three surviving Early Bronze Age standing stones (c. 2000 BC) aligned north-to-south over 374 metres near Boroughbridge, North Yorkshire. Fashioned from Millstone Grit quarried approximately 7 miles away at Knaresborough, they stand between 5.4 and 6.8 metres high, each weighing 20-30 tonnes. Their surfaces bear distinctive vertical grooves caused by millennia of rainfall erosion. Originally four or more stones, one was removed in the 18th century during a treasure hunt and reused for a bridge. A scheduled monument since 1923, the alignment may have served astronomical or ceremonial purposes.\" -> \"The Devil''s Arrows are three aligned standing stones located outside Boroughbridge in North Yorkshire, England, near to where the A168 road crosses the River Ure [1]. The outer stones are 360 and 200 feet away from the central stone and form an almost straight line, running NNW–SSE [1]. Erected during the Stone Age and distinctively grooved by millennia of rainfall, the tallest stone is 22.5 feet in height, making this the second tallest menhir in the United Kingdom after the 25 feet tall Rudston Monolith in the East Riding of Yorkshire [1]. In 2026, scientists at Curtin University and the University of York used isotopic fingerprinting to show that the stones originated at Brimham Rocks, about 11 miles to the west [1]. The study also found that the Battle Cross at Aldborough has the same geological fingerprint as the surviving Arrows, supporting its identification as a repurposed fourth stone from the original monument [1]. The stones are part of a prehistoric complex concentrated around the River Ure [1]. The name is mentioned by the antiquary John Aubrey, who visited and drew the stones in 1687 [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97290", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97291", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-3500'' -> ''-2000''"}]'::jsonb),
