n on a hill, utilizing steep slopes on its southern and northern sides as natural defenses [1]. The eastern approach, lacking the benefit of natural slopes, is protected by two defensive ramparts (earthwork banks) [1]. As an univallate structure, it represents a relatively simple but effective defensive design typical of Iron Age settlements in southern Britain [1].\" -> \"Wain''s Hill is an univallate Iron Age hill fort located approximately 1.6 km (1 mile) south-west of Clevedon in North Somerset, England [1]. The site occupies a naturally defensible position on a hill, utilizing steep slopes on its southern and northern sides as natural defenses [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385711", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385712", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:399681", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:399682", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start None -> ''-1500''"}]'::jsonb),
