 by a bank and ditch, lie south of Manor Farm. Excavations at Tower House Farm (1950-53) uncovered a Roman villa, and additional Roman features are scattered throughout the valley. The village also preserves earthworks from a deserted medieval settlement east of Wraxall House, with evidence of houses, enclosures, and possibly a watermill. The name Wraxall is thought to derive from ''a nook of land frequented by buzzards''.\" -> \"Wraxall is a village in North Somerset with archaeological heritage spanning multiple periods [1] [2]. Earthworks of an oval Iron Age settlement, surrounded by a bank and ditch, lie south of Manor Farm [1] [3]. Excavations (1950-53) uncovered a Roman villa, and additional Roman features are scattered throughout the valley [1] [2]. The village also preserves earthworks from a deserted medieval settlement, with evidence of houses, enclosures, and possibly a watermill [1]. The name Wraxall is thought to derive from ''a nook of land frequented by buzzards'' [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348159", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348160", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
