 containing several hundred Paleolithic campsites -- the world''s largest concentration of such finds. The site is centered on a hematite mine where ochre was extracted for ritual purposes via open-cast pits up to 1.5 m deep. Cultures represented include Hamburg (13,000-12,000 BP), Federmesser (11,800-10,800 BP), and Swiderian (10,800-10,000 BP). Discovered in 1921 by Ludwik Sawicki, the reserve was formally established in 1986 after Stefan Krukowski linked settlements to mining activities.\" -> \"Grzybowa Gora is part of the Rydno Archaeological Reserve in south-central Poland, a 10 km stretch along the Kamienna River valley containing several hundred Paleolithic campsites -- the world''s largest concentration of such finds [1] [2] [3]. The site is centered on a hematite mine where ochre was extracted for ritual purposes via open-cast pits up to 1.5 m deep [2]. Cultures represented include Hamburg (13,000-12,000 BP), Federmesser (11,800-10,800 BP), and Swiderian (10,800-10,000 BP) [2].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385733", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385734", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_start ''-3000000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:399701", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:399702", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start None -> ''-4501''"}]'::jsonb),
