    ('4b3024e4-d801-49ab-b0c4-d496090cee40'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:4b3024e4-d801-49ab-b0c4-d496090cee40:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Horvat Omrit', '342aa02592abfbd14ac1802e6f3e28b9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Temple complex'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 53 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34449", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0028:chunk-0001: description \"Horvat Omrit (Khirbat ''Umayrī) is an archaeological site featuring the ruins of a Roman temple complex[1]. Located on the western slopes of the Golan Heights where they meet the Upper Jordan Valley, the site lies within the 1949 Israel–Syria demilitarized zone[1]. The temple was likely constructed by Herod the Great in the early 1st century BC to honor the Roman emperor Augustus[2]. The complex was destroyed during the major Galilee earthquake of 363 AD[2]. In the subsequent Byzantine period, a small chapel was erected on the temple''s ruins[2]. The site represents one of the northernmost examples of Herodian architecture in the region.\" -> ''Omrit, or Khirbat ‘Umayrī, is the site of an ancient Roman temple [1]. It stands where the western slopes of the Golan Heights meet the Upper Jordan Valley [1]. The site was destroyed in the Galilee earthquake of 363; a small chapel was later built on its ruins in the Byzantine period [1]. Omrit is situated atop a foothill of Mount Hermon, overlooking Hula Lake [1]. The site is located approximately 2.5 miles (c. 4 km) southwest of Banias, adjacent to a Roman road connecting Scythopolis and Damascus [1]. After a brushfire cleared the area in 1998, archaeological excavations began, being led by Professor Andrew J. Overman of Macalester College [1]. The region was well trodden by Roman influence, and thus far excavations have yielded three phases of temple construction approximated at mid 1st century BCE, 20 BCE and 1st century CE [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:148727", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4b3024e4/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4b3024e4/המקדש_בעומרית_04.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333802", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4b3024e4/המקדש_בעומרית_04.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4b3024e4/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
