 The Sinagua people lived here from roughly 1100 to 1300 AD in an estimated 72-room complex housing around 200 people. Pictographs at the site predate the cliff dwellings, with some dating back to 2000 BC, while most rock art was created by the Sinagua between 900 and 1300 AD. Together with its sister site Palatki, Honanki represents the largest cliff dwellings in the Red Rock country.'' -> ''Honanki is a cliff dwelling and rock art site in Coconino National Forest, about 24 km west of Sedona, Arizona [1]. The Sinagua people lived here from roughly 1100 to 1300 AD in an estimated 72-room complex [1] [2]. Pictographs at the site predate the cliff dwellings, with some dating back to 2000 BC, while most rock art was created by the Sinagua between 900 and 1300 AD [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4d0fa83d-8685-49ee-ba7d-27d9f256e303'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:4d0fa83d-8685-49ee-ba7d-27d9f256e303:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Craig Rhiwarth', '2de0a203b68d229dfeb6104f4375ae40', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fort'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 62 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83772", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2085:chunk-0001: description ''Craig Rhiwarth is an Iron Age hillfort on a mountain in the Berwyn range, Powys, Wales, overlooking the village of Llangynog [1]. There are remains of slate quarrying visible on the southern slopes [1].'' -> ''Craig Rhiwarth is a mountain in the Berwyn range, in Powys (formerly Montgomeryshire), Wales [1]. On the summit is an Iron Age hillfort [1]. The altitude of Craig Rhiwarth is 532 metres; the hillfort is one of the highest in Wales [1]. The size of the enclosure is about 850 metres east to west by 400–500 metres [1]. The site has precipitous slopes on all sides except the north: on this side there is a ruinous stone wall above a scarp [1]. There is a simple entrance near the centre of the wall and a slanting entrance at the west end [1]. Within this area are the foundations of about 170 circular stone structures, of diameter 4–12 metres [1]. It is thought that the settlement may have begun in the late Bronze Age and continued into the Iron Age, and that its function probably ceased by the time of the Roman conquest in the 1st century A.D. There are also a few rectangular hafodydd, probably of medieval date, relating to seasonal settlement [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116424", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116425", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116426", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s003: site_type ''Fortress/citadel'' -> ''Fort''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:237570", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:237571", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-800''"}]'::jsonb),
