    ('521ade1a-0eaf-442b-973d-ccae9e9fbb35'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:521ade1a-0eaf-442b-973d-ccae9e9fbb35:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''7'' restored on Menhir of Meada', '39e3ae89667223e4dd907afac0023c90', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 12 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34499", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0029:chunk-0001: description \"The Menhir of Meada is believed to be the largest megalithic monument in the Iberian Peninsula entirely carved by humans[1]. Standing in the Serra de São Mamede Natural Park in Portugal''s Portalegre District, this massive monolith of coarse-grained porphyroid granite is situated 12 km north of Castelo de Vide[1].\" -> \"The Menhir of Meada is believed to be the largest menhir in the Iberian Peninsula that was entirely carved by humans [1]. It is situated 12 km to the north of Castelo de Vide in the Portalegre District of Portugal and is within the protected area of the Serra de São Mamede Natural Park [1]. The monolith, consisting of coarse-grained porphyroid granite, was discovered in a rural area, now used mainly for cork production, in 1965 [1]. Discovered in two pieces, it was restored in the 1990s, when the two parts were joined and the complete stone was replaced in its original location [1]. The menhir''s total length is about 7.5 metres, with a maximum diameter of 1.25 metres and a circumference of 3.9 metres at the base [1]. It weighs around 18 tons [1]. Based on radiocarbon dating of coals discovered underneath the base, the menhir is believed to have been raised around 5000 BCE, in the Neo-Chalcolithic period, approximately the same time as the Coureleiros dolmens, or megalithic tombs, that are found in the same district [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149547", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/521ade1a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/521ade1a/MenhirofMeada.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
