    ('5928dcb7-1a73-4bf6-8bd3-3fe6ba8c6b90'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:5928dcb7-1a73-4bf6-8bd3-3fe6ba8c6b90:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: antiquity ''5'' restored on Carnfree', 'f91cd4c763a2d311d0f334a43d40472d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -3000"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84668", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2093:chunk-0001: description \"Carnfree is a multi-period archaeological complex south of Tulsk in County Roscommon, located near the royal site of Rathcroghan [1]. The principal feature is a mound dating to 3000–2000 BC, traditionally identified as the cairn of the legendary Connacht warrior Fráech [1]. This Bronze Age funerary monument later served as an inauguration site for medieval kings of Connacht, continuing ancient ceremonial traditions into the late medieval period [1]. The complex also includes the area of Selc, featuring the passage tomb Duma Selga [1], and an early Christian ecclesiastical site associated with Saint Patrick''s ministry to the Ui Brian princes of Connacht [1].\" -> ''Carnfree is a site south of the village of Tulsk in Roscommon that also lies close to the more celebrated ancient landscape of Rathcroghan [1]. The chief feature here is the bronze-age mound of Carnfree itself, believed to be the cairn of the Connacht warrior Fráech, that was used as an inauguration place up to late medieval times [1]. Up until the mid-nineteenth century a proclamation stone stood nearby that featured two sunken footprin