    ('626e8712-71b4-48ac-9b58-8440bade0fcc'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:626e8712-71b4-48ac-9b58-8440bade0fcc:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''6'' restored on Savaria Mithraeum', '5c54ab8b8abcba8e82d9980bae9f2416', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Temple complex'', ''Undated'') is shared by 7 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84924", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2100:chunk-0001: description ''Savaria Mithraeum is a Roman shrine to the god Mithras in ancient Savaria (modern Szombathely, Hungary), discovered in 2008 during construction of a shopping center. The temple features stone altars decorated with relief scenes of Mithraic mythology, stone benches lining the sanctuary walls, and wall paintings using rare pigments including Egyptian Blue and vermilion. A major fire in the 4th century caused the structure to collapse, burying it underground for centuries. The find is considered of European importance for understanding Roman religious practices in Pannonia.'' -> ''Savaria Mithraeum was the shrine of Mithras in the Roman town of Savaria which was discovered in 2008 in Szombathely [1]. The Savaria Mithraeum was used until the 4th century when it was burned down and collapsed [1]. The mithraeum was discovered during the building works of a new shopping center in 2008 [1]. Two stone altars were uncovered which were decorated with scenes from the life of Mithras in reliefs and had dedicative inscriptions [1]. The ceiling was coffered [1].''"},