ngle rampart and ditch (univallate) following the contours of the hill top, enclosing an area of 6¾ acres (2.7 hectares) [1]. The main entrance is to the east, towards Nether Stowey, with a simpler opening to the north-west, aligned with a ridgeway leading down to Holford [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100110", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:100111", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s009: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151626", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6290ca43/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6290ca43/Dowsborough_Digital_Terrain_Model.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:238548", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:238549", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''-1000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:322988", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-004-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6290ca43/Dowsborough_Digital_Terrain_Model.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6290ca43/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:325245", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6290ca43/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6290ca43/Dowsborough seen from just north of Nether Stowey.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('629670bb-a4ec-4a5a-97a5-7623f86f46d8'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:629670bb-a4ec-4a5a-97a5-7623f86f46d8:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Asclepieion of Athens', '47677f06888947dc555a3a6e46926996', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Temple complex'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 53 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29549", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0306:chunk-0001: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-419''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30417", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80932", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2013:chunk-0001: description \"A healing sanctuary on the south slope of the Acropolis, founded in 420/419 BC by Telemachos of Acharnai during the Peloponnesian War, likely prompted by the Athenian plague. Dedicated to Asclepius and Hygieia, patients slept in a Doric stoa hoping the god would visit them in dreams and cure their ailments. The complex included a temple, altar, Ionic dining stoa, and a sacred spring. A marble stele known as the Telemachos Monument records the god''s arrival from Epidaurus.\" -> ''The Asclepieion of Athens was an ancient Greek temple in Athens built to honour of the gods Asclepius and Hygieia, located west of the Theatre of Dionysos and east of the Pelargikon wall on the southern escarpment of the Acropolis hill [1]. It later became a Christian church in the sixth century [1]. The Asclepieion was one of several asklepieia in the ancient Greek world that served as rudimentary hospitals [1]. It was founded in the year 419–18 BCE during the Peloponnesian War, perhaps as a direct result of the plague, by Telemachos Acharneas [1]. The sanctuary complex consisted of the temple and the altar of the god as well as two galleries, the Doric stoa which served as a katagogion, a space where visitors seeking healing would spend the night in the hope of seeing the god in their dreams and being healed by him, and the Ionic Stoa that served as a dining hall and lodging for the priests of Asclepius and their visitors [1]. The Doric stoa was built according to inscriptions in 300/299 BCE and was a two-storey building with 17 Doric columns on its façade [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151629", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/629670bb/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/629670bb/Asclepieion of Athens, 20240531 1336 9699.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312015", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/629670bb/Asclepieion of Athens, 20240531 1336 9699.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/629670bb/Asclepieion_(Athens)_01.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('629670bb-a4ec-4a5a-97a5-7623f86f46d8'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '30', '33', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:629670bb-a4ec-4a5a-97a5-7623f86f46d8:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: rarity_score ''33'' restored on Asclepieion of Athens', '47677f06888947dc555a3a6e46926996', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 30 from mystery 4, cultural_influence 8"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29549", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0306:chunk-0001: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-419''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30417", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80932", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2013:chunk-0001: description \"A healing sanctuary on the south slope of the Acropolis, founded in 420/419 BC by Telemachos of Acharnai during the Peloponnesian War, likely prompted by the Athenian plague. Dedicated to Asclepius and Hygieia, patients slept in a Doric stoa hoping the god would visit them in dreams and cure their ailments. The complex included a temple, altar, Ionic dining stoa, and a sacred spring. A marble stele known as the Telemachos Monument records the god''s arrival from Epidaurus.\" -> ''The Asclepieion of Athens was an ancient Greek temple in Athens built to honour of the gods Asclepius and Hygieia, located west of the Theatre of Dionysos and east of the Pelargikon wall on the southern escarpment of the Acropolis hill [1]. It later became a Christian church in the sixth century [1]. The Asclepieion was one of several asklepieia in the ancient Greek world that served as rudimentary hospitals [1]. It was founded in the year 419–18 BCE during the Peloponnesian War, perhaps as a direct result of the plague, by Telemachos Acharneas [1]. The sanctuary complex consisted of the temple and the altar of the god as well as two galleries, the Doric stoa which served as a katagogion, a space where visitors seeking healing would spend the night in the hope of seeing the god in their dreams and being healed by him, and the Ionic Stoa that served as a dining hall and lodging for the priests of Asclepius and their visitors [1]. The Doric stoa was built according to inscriptions in 300/299 BCE and was a two-storey building with 17 Doric columns on its façade [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151629", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/629670bb/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/629670bb/Asclepieion of Athens, 20240531 1336 9699.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312015", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/629670bb/Asclepieion of Athens, 20240531 1336 9699.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/629670bb/Asclepieion_(Athens)_01.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
