es (330 ft) above sea level[1].'' -> ''Windbury Head is the site of an Iron Age hill fort on the Hartland Peninsula in North Devon, England [1]. Most of the site has been lost to coastal erosion [1]. Only the southern ramparts survive today, standing at approximately 100 metres (330 ft) above sea level [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6c6bb00c-45f8-4d2e-8532-79e612ae267a'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:6c6bb00c-45f8-4d2e-8532-79e612ae267a:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''7'' restored on Menhir of Bulhoa', '6937e7ca70c9b4f5994698a94144a263', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29528", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0302:chunk-0001: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34875", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0035:chunk-0001: description ''A granite megalithic standing stone near Monsaraz in Reguengos de Monsaraz, Evora district, Portugal, dating to approximately 4000-2500 BC. Originally 4 metres tall, it was only identified in 1970 when found lying flat and fractured. After restoration it was re-erected, though it lost its original base which had to be recarved. Classified as a National Monument since 1970, the menhir features engraved circles and lines of uncertain meaning.'' -> \"The Menhir of Bulhoa (Portuguese: Menir da Bulhoa), also known as the Menhir of Abelhoa, is a granite megalithic standing stone, located near the parish of Monsaraz, in the municipality of the Reguengos de Monsaraz, in the Évora district of the Alentejo region of Portugal [1]. The menhir is thought to date back to between 3500 and 2500 BCE [1]. Overlooked for a long time, it was identified in 1970 [1]. At that time it was lying flat on the ground, with the upper part separated from its base, which had been used in a nearby grape press [1]. It was restored to its original shape soon after discovery, with the fracture remaining very noticeable [1]. The menhir is part of a megalithic nucleus that includes the menhirs of Monte da Ribeira and Outeiro and the Xerez Cromlech [1]. It has a phallic appearance, being about four meters high and one meter in diameter [1]. The stone is decorated with engravings depicting the sun''s rays, a staff, and wavy and zigzagging lines [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35820", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-3500'' -> ''-4500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152309", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6c6bb00c/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6c6bb00c/Menir da Bulhoa Reguengos de Monsaraz 1.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312175", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6c6bb00c/Menir da Bulhoa Reguengos de Monsaraz 1.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6c6bb00c/BulhoaMenhir1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
