t separated from its base, which had been used in a nearby grape press [1]. It was restored to its original shape soon after discovery, with the fracture remaining very noticeable [1]. The menhir is part of a megalithic nucleus that includes the menhirs of Monte da Ribeira and Outeiro and the Xerez Cromlech [1]. It has a phallic appearance, being about four meters high and one meter in diameter [1]. The stone is decorated with engravings depicting the sun''s rays, a staff, and wavy and zigzagging lines [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35820", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-3500'' -> ''-4500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152309", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-015: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6c6bb00c/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6c6bb00c/Menir da Bulhoa Reguengos de Monsaraz 1.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312175", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/6c6bb00c/Menir da Bulhoa Reguengos de Monsaraz 1.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6c6bb00c/BulhoaMenhir1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
