    ('72d00882-cac4-420f-8105-e1a0f7999340'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:72d00882-cac4-420f-8105-e1a0f7999340:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''7'' restored on Koumasa', 'a4c18177de6757ed6fc53c8eccf3888b', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cemetery'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88776", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2501:chunk-0001: description ''Koumasa is a Prepalatial Minoan cemetery on Crete. Located between Loukia and Koumasa at the southern edge of the Mesara Plain near the Asterousia Mountains [1]. First excavated by Stephanos Xanthoudides in 1904–1906, the cemetery was re-examined in 1991–1992 by Alexandra Karetsou and Athanasia Kanta [1]. Since 2012, Heidelberg University has conducted systematic excavations and digital mapping [2].'' -> ''Koumasa is a Minoan archaeological site in southern Crete, best known for its large prepalatial cemetery [1]. It is located between the villages of Loukia and Koumasa, at the southern edge of the Mesara Plain and near the foothills of the Asterousia Mountains [1]. The site was first excavated by Stephanos Xanthoudides between 1904 and 1906 [1]. Koumasa includes both a Prepalatial cemetery and an associated settlement extending across the saddle of the Korakies hill [1]. The site is also notable for its marble folded-arm figurines, which combine Cycladic features with local Minoan styles [1]. The burial architecture at Koumasa is dominated by large circular tholos tombs constructed during the Prepalatial Minoan period [1]. These tombs are built from locally sourced limestone, employing dry-stone construction techniques without the use of mortar [1]. Outside Tholos B, a secondary burial deposit yielded an assemblage of 140 multicolored beads, seven stone seals, gold foil bands, a small rock crystal phiale, obsidian blades, and bronze tools [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101688", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101689", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152961", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/72d00882/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/72d00882/Clay miniaure model of a wine-press, offering, Koumasa, 2300-1900 BC, AMH, 144788.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
