f Murujuga are an Aboriginal nation known as the Yaburara people [1]. Some of the Aboriginal rock carvings have been dated to more than 45,000 years old, and some of the collection is generally estimated to be 40,000–50,000 years old [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:118986", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: site_type ''Rock art'' -> ''Petroglyphs''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:118987", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murujuga'' -> ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burrup_Peninsula''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152974", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-016: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Pilbara_Billabong.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7356fc90/Ancient_and_Modern_4,000_years.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334532", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7356fc90/Ancient_and_Modern_4,000_years.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7356fc90/Pilbara_Billabong.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7356fc90-8d0c-4554-b51d-f6b65c926244'::uuid, 'total_power', '26', '27', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:7356fc90-8d0c-4554-b51d-f6b65c926244:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: total_power ''27'' restored on Murujuga', 'ce9bbfc8c6c9b46027147f14dddc62d1', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 26 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 10 + 2 + 7 + 6 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34947", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0037:chunk-0001: description \"Murujuga, also known as the Burrup Peninsula, is located in the Dampier Archipelago of Western Australia''s Pilbara region and contains the world''s largest and most significant collection of petroglyphs, estimated at over one million images spanning up to 50,000 years. The rock art includes depictions of the extinct thylacine and documents an unbroken record of human existence through the last ice age. Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2025, the cultural landscape is custodially managed by the Ngarluma, Yindjibarndi, Yaburara, Mardudhunera, and Wong-Goo-Tt-Oo peoples. Industrial development since 1963 has destroyed an estimated 24% of rock art on the peninsula.\" -> ''The Burrup Peninsula, previously known as Dampier Island, is a former island of the Dampier Archipelago that is now connected to the mainland via a causeway [1]. The peninsula and islands together are also known as Murujuga [1]. The peninsula is located in the Pilbara region of Western Australia and contains the town of Dampier as well as the Murujuga National Park [1]. The traditional owners of Murujuga are an Aboriginal nation known as the Yaburara people [1]. Some of the Aboriginal rock carvings have been dated to more than 45,000 years old, and some of the collection is generally estimated to be 40,000–50,000 years old [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:118986", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: site_type ''Rock art'' -> ''Petroglyphs''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:118987", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: source_url ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murujuga'' -> ''https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burrup_Peninsula''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:152974", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-016: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Pilbara_Billabong.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7356fc90/Ancient_and_Modern_4,000_years.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334532", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7356fc90/Ancient_and_Modern_4,000_years.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7356fc90/Pilbara_Billabong.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('739f3b71-4f87-4f30-9a58-de5e2c95f0af'::uuid, 'antiquity', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:739f3b71-4f87-4f30-9a58-de5e2c95f0af:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: antiquity ''5'' restored on Burgh Walls Camp', '80544c42d9a0df958878235befaf04be', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 6 from period_start -1500"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85328", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2111:chunk-0001: description ''Burgh Walls Camp is a multivallate Iron Age hill fort in North Somerset, England, dating from approximately 1500–500 BC [1]. Located within Leigh Woods about 2.6 km (1.6 mi) north-east of Long Ashton near Bristol, it sits above the banks of the River Avon [1]. The fort is one of three Iron Age fortifications overlooking the Avon Gorge, alongside Stokeleigh [1]. Alternative names include Bower Walls Camp, Burwalls, and Bowre Walls [1].'' -> ''Burgh Walls Camp is a multivallate Iron Age hill fort in the North Somerset district of Somerset [1]. The hill fort is situated within Leigh Woods approximately 1.6 miles (2.6 km) north-east from the village of Long Ashton near Bristol, above the banks of the River Avon [1]. The hillfort has some alternative names such as Bower Walls Camp, Burwalls, or Bowre Walls [1]. Burgh Walls Camp is one of three Iron Age fortifications overlooking the Avon Gorge, the others being Stokeleigh Camp and Clifton Camp on the opposite side of the gorge, on Clifton Down near the Observatory [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101696", "url": "remediation_change_l