n in 1980 and excavations from 1983-1985 revealed a Neronian auxiliary fort established c. AD 55, measuring approximately 105 by 170 metres (1.8 hectares) with triple ditches on the east side. The fort served as part of a defensive screen protecting Legio II Augusta at Exeter, alongside forts at Okehampton and North Tawton. A large pre-Flavian fort was succeeded by a smaller one, both abandoned early in the Flavian period.'' -> ''Near the village of Lapford in Mid Devon lies the Bury Barton Roman fort, a Scheduled Monument first identified by Professor St Joseph in 1975 [1] [2] [3]. Ground examination in 1980 and excavations from 1983-1985 revealed a Neronian auxiliary fort established c. AD 55, measuring approximately 105 by 170 metres (1.8 hectares) with triple ditches on the east side [2]. The fort served as part of a defensive screen protecting Legio II Augusta at Exeter, alongside forts at Okehampton and North Tawton [2] [4].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349956", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s005: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:349957", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s005: period_start ''1'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('758dd394-0b33-4f82-821d-5b6de5058afe'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:758dd394-0b33-4f82-821d-5b6de5058afe:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''7'' restored on Aubrey Holes - Stonehenge', '57a25ed3558bfb1206b787b34a1685b0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cemetery'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32747", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0002:chunk-0001: description \"Ring of 56 chalk pits forming the earliest phase of Stonehenge (c. 3000 BC), first noted by antiquarian John Aubrey in 1666 and formally identified by Robert Newall during Colonel Hawley''s 1920s excavations. Each pit averages 0.76 m deep and 1.06 m in diameter. A 2008 excavation by Mike Parker Pearson suggested the holes originally held Welsh bluestones, based on their identical width and shape to bluestones found elsewhere at the monument. Human cremation burials were found within and around most holes, indicating a funerary function.\" -> \"The Aubrey holes are a ring of 56 chalk pits at Stonehenge, named after seventeenth-century antiquarian John Aubrey [1]. They date to the earliest phases of Stonehenge in the late fourth and early third millennium BC [1]. 25 of the holes were excavated by Hawley in 1920 and seven more in 1924 [1]. In 1950 Stuart Piggott and Richard Atkinson dug two more Aubrey Holes which brought the total excavated to 35, including one that Richard Colt Hoare may have encountered whilst digging beneath the fallen Slaughter Stone in the early nineteenth century [1]. It was found that the pits were an average of 0.76m deep and 1.06m in diameter [1]. 25 of the pits contained later cremation burials inserted into their upper fills along with long bone pins which may have secured leather or cloth bags used to hold the remains [1]. Their presence makes Stonehenge Britain''s oldest cremation cemetery [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:119006", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:119007", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:119008", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: site_type ''Timber circle'' -> ''Cemetery''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384267", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4401:chunk-0001: description \"The Aubrey holes are a ring of 56 chalk pits at Stonehenge, named after seventeenth-century antiquarian John Aubrey [1]. They date to the earliest phases of Stonehenge in the late fourth and early third millennium BC [1]. 25 of the holes were excavated by Hawley in 1920 and seven more in 1924 [1]. In 1950 Stuart Piggott and Richard Atkinson dug two more Aubrey Holes which brought the total excavated to 35, including one that Richard Colt Hoare may have encountered whilst digging beneath the fallen Slaughter Stone in the early nineteenth century [1]. It was found that the pits were an average of 0.76m deep and 1.06m in diameter [1]. 25 of the pits contained later cremation burials inserted into their upper fills along with long bone pins which may have secured leather or cloth bags used to hold the remains [1]. Their presence makes Stonehenge Britain''s oldest cremation cemetery [1].\" -> ''The Aubrey holes are a ring of 56 chalk pits at Stonehenge, named after seventeenth-century antiquarian John Aubrey [1] [2]. They date to the earliest phases of Stonehenge in the late fourth and early third millennium BC [3] [4].''"}]'::jsonb),
