 about 1 mile west of the village of Llangedwyn, in Powys [1]. The hillfort has a single rampart, about 264 metres north to south and 204 metres east to west, following the contours of a prominent hill [1]. The area is about 3 hectares [1]. There is a simple inturned entrance on the south-east [1]. There was excavation in 1983 [1]. It was found that the site was first occupied in the late Bronze Age, about the 10th–9th century BC; the defences were enlarged about the 4th–3rd century BC, during the Iron Age [1]. Built into the rear of the rampart was an Iron Age smithy, for bronze-casting and iron-working, probably to serve the requirements of the settlement [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153728", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-017: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7a0bddc9/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7a0bddc9/Llwyn Bryn-Dinas 1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7abb834a-305d-43b5-b0fe-283261d83cf0'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:7abb834a-305d-43b5-b0fe-283261d83cf0:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Temple of Victory, Himera', 'af0f8b5b706f2689dfd0ab0d17dafbf4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Temple complex'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 53 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85504", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2115:chunk-0001: description ''The Temple of Victory (Greek: Nikē) is an ancient Greek temple in the archaeological area of Termini Imerese, Sicily, southern Italy[1]. The temple dates to between 480 and 470 BC and was built following the Greek victory over Carthaginian forces at the Battle of Himera in 480 BC[1]. The temple was dedicated to the goddess Athena[1]. Its ruins are situated in the village of Buonfornello, within the municipality of Termini Imerese[1].'' -> ''The Temple of Victory is a Greek temple of the ancient city of Himera in Magna Graecia located in the archaeological area of Termini Imerese, in the Metropolitan City of Palermo in Sicily [1]. The temple dates to between 480 and 470 BC and has been identified with the temple built at the order of the tyrant Gelon of Syracuse who commanded the Greek coalition which defeated a Carthaginian invasion force at the Battle of Himera in 480 BC [1]. Probably dedicated to Athena, the building was burnt and destroyed, most likely in 409 BC when the Carthaginians captured the city of Himera [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153736", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-017: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/Himera_Viktoriatempel_Detail.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7abb834a/Himera_Viktoriatempel_Detail.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
