    ('880170da-bad3-44c5-a994-0bf644eb4ead'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:880170da-bad3-44c5-a994-0bf644eb4ead:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''7'' restored on Chalandriani', '0f8066f938a929eb6059c0bc36ed0806', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cemetery'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85824", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2124:chunk-0001: description ''Chalandriani is the largest Early Cycladic cemetery yet discovered, located on the island of Syros in Greece and dated to the Early Cycladic II period (Keros-Syros culture, 2600-2300 BC). First excavated by Christos Tsountas in 1898, the site contains over 600 tombs of the subterranean corbelled type. Grave goods include pottery with engraved ship decorations, Cycladic marble figurines, silver and bronze artifacts, and marble vessels. The fortified prehistoric settlement of Kastri, with a double stone fortification wall 77 yards long, lies immediately north of the cemetery. The site was declared a protected archaeological zone in 1992.'' -> ''Chalandriani is a major Early Bronze Age cemetery on the Cycladic island of Syros in Greece, a little way to the south of the fortified prehistoric settlement of Kastri [1]. Most of the tombs at Chalandriani were constructed before the settlement of Kastri was first inhabited [1]. Among the grave goods discovered at Chalandriani are \"frying pans\", several of which are decorated with longboat motifs almost unknown from other sites [1]. Unlike most Early Cycladic tombs, which are not generally oriented to the compass in a consistent manner, the tombs at Chalandriani all faced between northwest and northeast [1]. Between 1898 and 1899, Christos Tsountas excavated the cemetery of Chalandriani and the settlement of Kastri on behalf of the Archaeological Society of Athens [1]. The American archaeologist John Caskey visited Chalandriani in the early 1960s, correctly dating the cemetery to the Early Cycladic II period [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:154746", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-018: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/880170da/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/880170da/Frying pan 080747.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313863", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/880170da/Frying pan 080747.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/880170da/Idol_EC_II_NAMA_080769.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
