on the Acropolis [1]. It has a prominent position on a steep bastion at the southwest corner of the Acropolis to the right of the entrance, the Propylaea [1]. The Sanctuary of Athena Nike was demolished by the Persians in 480-479 BC and a temple was built over the remains [1]. The new temple construction was underway in 449 BC and was finished around 420 BC [1]. The Temple of Athena Nike, the smallest on the Acropolis, was finished around 420 BC [1]. It is a tetrastyle Ionic structure with a colonnaded portico at both front and rear facades, designed by the architect Kallikrates [1]. The friezes of the building''s entablature were decorated on all sides with relief sculpture in the idealized classical style of the 5th century BC [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155369", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Temple_of_Athena_Nik%C3%A8_from_Propylaea%2C_Acropolis%2C_Athens%2C_Greece.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8d8753ae/Nike_adjusting_sandal.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313934", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8d8753ae/Nike_adjusting_sandal.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8d8753ae/Temple_of_Athena_Nikè_from_Propylaea,_Acropolis,_Athens,_Greece.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
