    ('903285ef-5216-4d41-88ee-8d386d6865f3'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:903285ef-5216-4d41-88ee-8d386d6865f3:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Temple of Dakka', 'bd66da5a2692bc2749f69cf90948231a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Temple complex'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 53 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:91882", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2212:chunk-0001: description ''The Temple of Dakka is a Greco-Roman temple complex in Lower Nubia, Egypt, dedicated to Thoth, the god of wisdom [1]. It began as a small one-room shrine in the 3rd century BC, commissioned by Meroitic king Arqamani in collaboration with Ptolemy IV, who added an antechamber and gate [1]. Ptolemy IX later enlarged it with a pronaos featuring two rows of columns [1]. During the Roman period, emperors Augustus and Tiberius added a second sanctuary containing a granite naos, along with inner and outer enclosure walls and a large pylon [1]. The Romans also transformed the temple into a fortress, surrounding it with a stone wall measuring 270 by 444 metres with an entrance along the Nile [1].'' -> \"The temple of Dakka collapsed in 1908–1909 and was subsequently rebuilt by Alessandro Barsanti via Anastylosis [1]. During the construction of the Aswan Dam in the 1960s, the temple was dismantled and moved to the site of New Wadi es-Sebua [1]. When the temple was rebuilt at New Wadi es-Sebua, additional newly identified original fragments were added [1]. At the time of the temple''s removal, some reused stone blocks from Thutmose III, Seti I and Merneptah were discovered which originated from an earlier New Kingdom structure in or near Kubban [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155417", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/The_Temple_of_Dakka_by_Dennis_Jarvis.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/903285ef/The_Temple_of_Dakka_by_Dennis_Jarvis.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
