0–35,000 BP), and Périgordian (35,000–20,000 BP) cultures [1]. Complex Mousterian burial structures found at La Ferrasie finally provided the evidence of Neanderthal burial practice [1]. At least seven Neanderthals have been found in La Ferrassie, including infants and one fetus [1]. All specimens were found in a thin 60 cm archaeological layer dated to 74-68 thousand years ago [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:103483", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s013: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:103484", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s013: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155916", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-020: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/90923e91/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/90923e91/La Ferrassie Grabungsflächen 2017-09-17.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251059", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s013: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251060", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s013: period_start None -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313999", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/90923e91/La Ferrassie Grabungsflächen 2017-09-17.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/90923e91/La_Ferrassie.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350888", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350889", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('90bd1f84-149e-4ee7-98c4-9c14b56298f1'::uuid, 'antiquity', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:90bd1f84-149e-4ee7-98c4-9c14b56298f1:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: antiquity ''5'' restored on Broch of West Burrafirth', 'a725036a8e3a7680cf043ea4e554dad0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 6 from period_start -1500"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83096", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26c_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83097", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26c_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85998", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2130:chunk-0001: description ''Iron Age broch on the Holm of Hebrista, a low rocky islet in West Burra Firth on Mainland Shetland, accessible only by boat or at very low tide. The ruined structure retains a blocked entrance with two flanking guard cells and four internal mural cells, two of which are distinctive dumb-bell shapes connected by short passages. The Norse named the surrounding inlet Borgarfjord (fjord of the borg) after the broch, a name recorded in a 1299 document in Old Norse. The site is a scheduled monument.'' -> ''The Broch of West Burrafirth is an Iron Age broch located on the west side of Mainland, Shetland [1]. The broch stands on a low rocky islet (the \"Holm of Hebrista\") in West Burra Firth [1]. The broch is in a ruined state with much fallen debris [1]. The entrance is blocked with stones but the lintel over the inner end is still apparent [1]. There appear to be two guard cells on either side of the entrance passage [1]. Internally four mural cells can be seen, two of which are dumb-bell shaped with short passages between the two halves [1]. Borgarfjörð, the \"fjord of the borg\" was so named by the Norse on account of the broch [1]. The broch was visited by George Low in the 18th century, when he noted a number of cells in the base of the wall, and he stated that a hollow, galleried wall began above these [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251063", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s013: period_name None -> ''500 - 1000 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251064", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s013: period_start None -> ''800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350892", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350893", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_start ''800'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:401129", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:401130", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_start None -> ''-1500''"}]'::jsonb),
