    ('972a2d53-376a-4f73-98bb-7a408040de58'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:972a2d53-376a-4f73-98bb-7a408040de58:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''8'' restored on Texcotzingo', '4bf2f15db94996088dfb5b459ad3a6a9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Castle/palace'', ''1000 - 1500 AD'') is shared by 6 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28825", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0198:chunk-0001: site_type ''Castle/palace'' -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35894", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: site_type ''Archaeological site'' -> ''Castle/palace''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86186", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2135:chunk-0001: description ''Texcotzingo is a 15th-century Aztec hilltop complex of botanical gardens, baths, and a royal retreat located about 32 km northeast of Mexico City. Designed by the poet-king Nezahualcoyotl of Texcoco, it features an ingenious aqueduct system that transported water from a spring near Mount Tlaloc to fill pools and irrigate terraced gardens. Three principal baths represent the capitals of Texcoco, Tula, and Tenayuca, with carved frog statues and depictions of rain deity Tlaloc. Considered possibly the oldest surviving pre-Columbian garden site in the Americas, the lower terraces housed medicinal and ornamental plants gathered from across the Aztec Empire.'' -> \"Texcotzingo is claimed to be one of the first extant botanical gardens in the world, along with Moctezuma''s gardens in Huastepec [1]. The gardens and archaeological site are located roughly 20 miles northeast of central Mexico City, Mexico [1]. Texcotzingo is adjacent to the Aztec capital city of Texcoco and acted as the summer imperial gardens including imperial and courtly residences and fantastic waterworks [1]. Texcotzingo was designed and created by Nezahualcoyotl, the then-ruler of Texcoco, in the 15th century [1]. New hydraulic projects and terrace gardens transformed the previously un-arable land into lush edible gardens containing the three ubiquitous staples of the Americas: maize, beans, and squash [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156142", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-020: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/El_invernadero_de_Nezahualcoyotl.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/972a2d53/Texcotzingo_Second_Level_View.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314107", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/972a2d53/Texcotzingo_Second_Level_View.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/972a2d53/El_invernadero_de_Nezahualcoyotl.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
