orld, along with Moctezuma''s gardens in Huastepec [1]. The gardens and archaeological site are located roughly 20 miles northeast of central Mexico City, Mexico [1]. Texcotzingo is adjacent to the Aztec capital city of Texcoco and acted as the summer imperial gardens including imperial and courtly residences and fantastic waterworks [1]. Texcotzingo was designed and created by Nezahualcoyotl, the then-ruler of Texcoco, in the 15th century [1]. New hydraulic projects and terrace gardens transformed the previously un-arable land into lush edible gardens containing the three ubiquitous staples of the Americas: maize, beans, and squash [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156142", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-020: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/El_invernadero_de_Nezahualcoyotl.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/972a2d53/Texcotzingo_Second_Level_View.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314107", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/972a2d53/Texcotzingo_Second_Level_View.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/972a2d53/El_invernadero_de_Nezahualcoyotl.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
