    ('9d1f90a0-7c45-4903-a83c-7503daf3db1b'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:9d1f90a0-7c45-4903-a83c-7503daf3db1b:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Temple of Hephaestus', '3d6afb280678729bbf6ab1d51abbfb4a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Temple complex'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 53 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44875", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0101:chunk-0001: description ''The best-preserved ancient Greek temple, standing largely intact atop the Agoraios Kolonos hill overlooking the Ancient Agora of Athens. Built between 449 and 415 BC, this Doric peripteral temple features a hexastyle arrangement of 6-by-13 columns in Pentelic marble, with eastern metopes depicting the Labours of Heracles. Dedicated to Hephaestus, god of metalworking, and Athena Ergane, it stood near the metalworking and pottery workshops of ancient Athens. Its conversion to the Church of Saint George Akamates from the 7th century until 1834 ensured its exceptional survival.'' -> ''The Temple of Hephaestus or Hephaisteion (also \"Hephesteum\" or \"Hephaesteum\"; Ancient Greek: Ἡφαιστεῖον, Greek: Ναός Ηφαίστου, and formerly called in error the Theseion or \"Theseum\"; Ancient Greek: Θησεῖον, Greek: Θησείο), is a well-preserved Greek temple dedicated to Hephaestus; it remains standing largely intact today [1]. It is a Doric peripteral temple, and is located at the north-west side of the Agora of Athens, on top of the Agoraios Kolonos hill [1]. Archaeological evidence suggests that there was no earlier building on the site except for a small sanctuary that was burned during the Second Persian invasion of Greece in 480 BC [1]. Construction started in 449 BC, and some scholars believe the building not to have been completed for some three decades [1]. The dimensions of the temple are 13.71 m north to south and 31.78 m east to west [1]. Only 18 of the 68 external metopes were sculpted; ten on the east front showed the Labors of Heracles [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:156910", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-021: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Hephaistos_Temple.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/9d1f90a0/Temple_of_Hephaestus_viewed_from_the_Stoa_of_Attalos_in_Athens,_Greece.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
