    ('a06ad29e-06f0-450b-bf8e-cd3b7231f096'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:a06ad29e-06f0-450b-bf8e-cd3b7231f096:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''6'' restored on The Longstones', '1b64fdda193b51657692a2bda1353599', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 28 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36999", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0048:chunk-0001: description \"Two prehistoric standing stones known as ''Adam'' and ''Eve'' near Beckhampton in Avebury parish, Wiltshire. Adam, the larger stone weighing an estimated 62 tons, is the surviving component of a Neolithic cove similar to the one in Avebury''s north circle. Eve likely formed part of the Beckhampton Avenue, a curving stone avenue connecting to Avebury that was long debated until archaeological investigations at the turn of the 21st century uncovered 120 metres of avenue socket holes in double rows. The cove appears aligned to the mid-winter sunrise. In 1912, Maud Cunnington re-erected Adam after it fell in 1911 and discovered a Beaker-period burial of a middle-aged man nearby.\" -> \"The Longstones are two standing stones, one of which is the remains of a prehistoric ''cove'' of standing stones, at grid reference SU089693, close to Beckhampton in Avebury parish [1]. Two stones are visible, known as ''Adam'' and ''Eve'', although the latter is more likely to have been a stone that formed part of the Beckhampton Avenue that connected with Avebury [1]. Adam is the larger of the two stones, weighing an estimated 62 tons, and along with three others formed a four-sided cove [1]. Excavations carried out jointly by the Universities of Leicester, Newport and Southampton in 2000 revealed the socket holes for the other stones which were tightly placed close to Adam [1]. The cove had been open on its south-eastern side which faced towards the nearby South Street barrow, 130m away [1]. The other stones were removed in the post-medieval period by a local landowner [1]. Adam fell over in 1911 and was re-erected by Maud Cunnington in 1912, at the direction of the Wiltshire Archaeological Society [1]. She also found a Beaker inhumation of a middle-aged man buried close by the stone [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105056", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105057", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2900''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Longstones%2C_Geograph.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314888", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/Longstones, Geograph.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384333", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4402:chunk-0001: description \"The Longstones are two standing stones, one of which is the remains of a prehistoric ''cove'' of standing stones, at grid reference SU089693, close to Beckhampton in Avebury parish [1]. Two stones are visible, known as ''Adam'' and ''Eve'', although the latter is more likely to have been a stone that formed part of the Beckhampton Avenue that connected with Avebury [1]. Adam is the larger of the two stones, weighing an estimated 62 tons, and along with three others formed a four-sided cove [1]. Excavations carried out jointly by the Universities of Leicester, Newport and Southampton in 2000 revealed the socket holes for the other stones which were tightly placed close to Adam [1]. The cove had been open on its south-eastern side which faced towards the nearby South Street barrow, 130m away [1]. The other stones were removed in the post-medieval period by a local landowner [1]. Adam fell over in 1911 and was re-erected by Maud Cunnington in 1912, at the direction of the Wiltshire Archaeological Society [1]. She also found a Beaker inhumation of a middle-aged man buried close by the stone [1].\" -> \"Two stones are visible, known as ''Adam'' and ''Eve'', although the latter is more likely to have been a stone that formed part of the Beckhampton Avenue that connected with Avebury [1] [2].\""}]'::jsonb),
