    ('a0b08567-cd34-467a-bd6d-428196261423'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '2', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:a0b08567-cd34-467a-bd6d-428196261423:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''2'' restored on Kuhikugu', 'dc941a340b6b74bf9054b0c36cce053e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''Undated'') is shared by 120 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37003", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0048:chunk-0001: description ''Kuhikugu is a pre-Columbian archaeological complex at the headwaters of the Xingu River in the Amazon rainforest of Brazil, discovered by anthropologist Michael Heckenberger in 1993 while working with Kuikuro indigenous guides. The complex encompasses around twenty towns and villages across 20,000 km2, supporting an estimated 30,000-50,000 people from roughly 1,500 to 400 years ago. Settlements featured circular plazas, defensive ditches, palisades, and roads 10-50 meters wide connecting communities. Inhabitants practiced manioc farming and fish farming using constructed dams and enriched terra preta soils.'' -> ''Kuhikugu is an archaeological site located in Brazil, at the headwaters of the Xingu River, in the Amazon rainforest [1]. Kuhikugu was first uncovered by anthropologist Michael Heckenberger, working alongside the local Kuikuro people, who are the likely descendants of the original inhabitants of Kuhikugu [1]. Kuhikugu was likely inhabited from a period of time around 1,500 years ago to a time as recently as 400 years ago, when the people living there were likely killed by diseases brought over by Europeans [1]. Large defensive ditches and palisades were built around some of the communities at Kuhikugu [1]. Many of the communities at Kuhikugu were linked, with roads which bridged some rivers along their paths, and with canoe canals running alongside some of the roads [1]. Sites X35 and X34 are significant communities connected by two of these roads [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105066", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105067", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157562", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a0b08567/hero.webp'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251523", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s014: period_name None -> ''500 - 1000 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251524", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s014: period_start None -> ''500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350987", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350988", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_start ''500'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
