    ('a1118dbb-9f77-4aae-a1d9-7fd43a7b29ec'::uuid, 'mystery', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:a1118dbb-9f77-4aae-a1d9-7fd43a7b29ec:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''4'' restored on Caterthuns', 'e32f4bba6b501a79854ab6c594e6680c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 1 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 235 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86422", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2141:chunk-0001: description ''Two Iron Age hillforts near Brechin in Angus, Scotland, situated about one kilometre apart on a ridge overlooking Strathmore, dating to c. 750-200 BC. The White Caterthun features an oval dry-stone wall originally several metres high and twelve metres thick, showing evidence of vitrification where intense heat fused the rocks into a glass-like substance. A well or cistern occupies its centre. The Brown Caterthun has five or six earthen embankments with nine radiating entrances and shows evidence of periodic burning. Both forts display very little evidence of permanent settlement, leaving their purpose -- military, mercantile, or ceremonial -- uncertain. They are designated as a scheduled monument.'' -> ''Caterthun, or the Caterthuns, is a ridge of hills near the city of Brechin in Angus [1]. The Caterthuns are notable for being the site of two Iron Age forts known as the White Caterthun and the Brown Caterthun which are designated as a scheduled monument [1]. The White Caterthun, on the west, is dominated by an oval fort consisting of a massive dry-stone wall, with a well or cistern in the middle [1]. The light-coloured stone wall gives the White Caterthun its name [1]. The Brown Caterthun, on the east, consists of a series of earthen embankments [1]. There is little evidence of settlement, agriculture or water supply here, so the purpose of the earthworks is uncertain [1]. Both Caterthuns show several entrances to the summit that radiate outwards, like the spokes on a wheel [1]. From radio-carbon dating, the Brown Caterthun appears to have been built and modified over several centuries in the latter half of the first millennium BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157576", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a1118dbb/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a1118dbb/White_Caterthun.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
