    ('a29d5e41-b7ef-4f9d-baf2-33c562ba9273'::uuid, 'mystery', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:a29d5e41-b7ef-4f9d-baf2-33c562ba9273:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''4'' restored on Teysheba', '07f3950c2968edf37bc50f1d35841e1d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 1 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 235 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86464", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2143:chunk-0001: description ''Odzaberd, originally named Teyseba after the Urartian thunder god, is a fortress situated on a hill east of Tsovinar at the southeast corner of Lake Sevan in Armenia\\''s Gegharkunik Province. Founded by Urartian king Rusa I between 735 and 713 BC, it is considered the best-preserved Urartian fortification in the Sevan basin. Recent Armenian-Italian excavations beginning in 2015 uncovered semi-cyclopean construction, multiple clay floors, pottery, and a nearby necropolis spanning the 10th to 6th centuries BC. In the medieval period it was renamed Odzaberd (\"Serpent\\''s Fortress\") after a local lord destroyed its defences.'' -> ''Odzaberd was an ancient settlement in modern Armenia on the shore of Lake Sevan [1]. It was founded in the 8th century BCE by a ruler of Urartu, an Iron Age kingdom centered in the Armenian Highlands, and continuously occupied into the Middle Ages [1]. The site is organized into three distinct zones: a central citadel, a fortress, and an extensive outer town [1]. A stele on the site bears an Urartian-language cuneiform inscription which states that Odzaberd was established by the Urartian king Rusa I (r. 735–714 BCE) in honor of the weather god Theispas to \"demonstrate the power of Biaina [the Urartian capital, modern Van] and to cow her enemies.\" Some sources refer to the site as \"Teisheba\" or other variations of the Urartian god\\''s name [1]. In the post-Urartian period Odzaberd was a regional center, as evidenced by recovered pottery styles and the use of mudbricks [1]. The Odzaberd site is on the southern shore of Lake Sevan near the town of Tsovinar [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121646", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s007: lat ''40.168024646758'' -> ''40.1528''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121647", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s007: lon ''45.45972198440738'' -> ''45.4953''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157638", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Overlooking_Teyseba.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a29d5e41/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:325320", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a29d5e41/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a29d5e41/Overlooking Teyseba.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
