t part of the English Channel to the important religious complexes of Avebury and Stonehenge, in Wiltshire, where it is known as the Harroway [1]. The way then existed as \"broad and ill-defined corridors of movement up to half a mile wide\" and not as a single, well-defined track [1]. The route was still followed as an artery for through traffic in Roman times [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105216", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105217", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:158393", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-023: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Pilgrims%27_Way_to_the_South_of_the_White_Horse_Stone_%28I%29.jpg'' -> \"/data/images/wiki/a98dcb51/Pilgrims'' Way to the South of the White Horse Stone (II).webp\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241633", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241634", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: period_start None -> ''-600''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315646", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url \"/data/images/wiki/a98dcb51/Pilgrims'' Way to the South of the White Horse Stone (II).webp\" -> \"/data/images/wiki/a98dcb51/Pilgrims''_Way_to_the_South_of_the_White_Horse_Stone_(I).webp\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('a996a5d6-dd44-441f-9616-b912c06f7f75'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '2', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:a996a5d6-dd44-441f-9616-b912c06f7f75:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''2'' restored on Pajaral', '430552a8873ee728b2eca7bbc4ec39d1', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''Undated'') is shared by 120 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86620", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2147:chunk-0001: description ''Pajaral is a Classic Maya archaeological site in the Peten department of Guatemala, identified as a capital of the ancient Hixwitz (\"Jaguar Hill\") kingdom. Previously known only from references at Yaxchilan and Piedras Negras, the kingdom\\''s location was confirmed through monument discoveries at Pajaral and nearby Zapote Bobal. The site features a large isolated hill with a monumental staircase and acropolis, likely the original \"Jaguar Hill\". An Early Classic stela fragment depicts a ruler named Yajawte\\'' K\\''inich, a name reused by a later Pajaral king, suggesting dynastic continuity.'' -> ''Pajaral, otherwise known as El Pajaral, is the modern name for a mid-sized ruined city of the pre-Columbian Maya archaeological site located to the south of the San Pedro Martir river in the Petén department of Guatemala [1]. The name El Pajaral was coined by archaeologist Ian Graham, who discovered the site in the 1970s, and refers to the numerous birds he encountered there during his survey [1]. The immediately visible architecture at Pajaral is characterized by temple-mounds ranging from between 8 and 20 metres in height [1]. Some of these are built atop natural hills: the upper plaza at the site was constructed on a modified hill 300 metres long by 30 metres high [1]. However, the architecture at the heart of Pajaral is massive: for example, the Main Plaza at Pajaral is accessed by a well-preserved, enormous staircase 46 metres wide and perhaps 30 metres long [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105218", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105219", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105220", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241636", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: lat ''16.902719920239637'' -> ''17.13015''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241637", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: lon ''-89.90071602546777'' -> ''-90.44553''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:241638", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s008: site_type None -> ''City''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251925", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251926", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_start None -> ''1''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:351796", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s007: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:351797", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s007: period_start ''1'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:3