    ('b539ba9e-5d7e-4ce8-a9ff-943ac3247297'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:b539ba9e-5d7e-4ce8-a9ff-943ac3247297:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''7'' restored on Carnac Stones', 'a75957534e83a0b4ac8ac87535b93cf3', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86940", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2156:chunk-0001: description \"The Carnac Stones are the largest collection of megalithic monuments in the world, comprising over 3,000 standing stones erected between the 5th and 3rd millennia BC by Neolithic farming communities in Brittany. The alignments stretch across 15 kilometres, with the most complete group at Le Menec featuring eleven parallel rows of stones decreasing in size eastward, terminating in stone circles. Their purpose remains debated, with theories ranging from processional ways and sacred spaces to astronomical calendars marking solstice sunrise patterns. Scientific study began in the 19th century with Scotsman J. Miln, whose student Zacharie Le Rouzic continued excavations and ensured the stones'' preservation.\" -> \"The Carnac stones are an exceptionally dense collection of megalithic sites near the south coast of Brittany in northwestern France, consisting of stone alignments, dolmens, tumuli, and single menhirs [1]. Most of the stones are within the Breton municipality of Carnac, but some to the east are within neighboring La Trinité-sur-Mer [1]. The standing stones are made of weathered granite from local outcroppings that once extensively covered the area [1]. Three exceptionally large burial mounds are known from the Carnac and Morbihan area, dating from the mid-5th millennium BC and known collectively as ''Carnacéen tumuli'': Saint-Michel, Tumiac and Mané-er-Hroëk [1]. Each of these tumuli contained a megalithic burial chamber, containing the burial of only one individual, along with numerous large polished stone axeheads, stone arm-rings, and jewellery made from callaïs [1]. Scientific analyses have shown that many of the axeheads are made of jade from the Italian Alps, whilst the callaïs was imported from south-western Iberia [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159969", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-025: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/Mégalithes_vus_depuis_la_Maison_des_Mégalithes_à_Carnac_le_1er_septembre_2019_-_2.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315858", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/Mégalithes_vus_depuis_la_Maison_des_Mégalithes_à_Carnac_le_1er_septembre_2019_-_2.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/Carnac_megalith_alignment_1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
