re made of weathered granite from local outcroppings that once extensively covered the area [1]. Three exceptionally large burial mounds are known from the Carnac and Morbihan area, dating from the mid-5th millennium BC and known collectively as ''Carnacéen tumuli'': Saint-Michel, Tumiac and Mané-er-Hroëk [1]. Each of these tumuli contained a megalithic burial chamber, containing the burial of only one individual, along with numerous large polished stone axeheads, stone arm-rings, and jewellery made from callaïs [1]. Scientific analyses have shown that many of the axeheads are made of jade from the Italian Alps, whilst the callaïs was imported from south-western Iberia [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159969", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-025: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/Mégalithes_vus_depuis_la_Maison_des_Mégalithes_à_Carnac_le_1er_septembre_2019_-_2.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315858", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/Mégalithes_vus_depuis_la_Maison_des_Mégalithes_à_Carnac_le_1er_septembre_2019_-_2.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/Carnac_megalith_alignment_1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b539ba9e-5d7e-4ce8-a9ff-943ac3247297'::uuid, 'total_power', '33', '34', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:b539ba9e-5d7e-4ce8-a9ff-943ac3247297:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: total_power ''34'' restored on Carnac Stones', 'a75957534e83a0b4ac8ac87535b93cf3', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 33 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 8 + 8 + 10 + 6 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86940", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2156:chunk-0001: description \"The Carnac Stones are the largest collection of megalithic monuments in the world, comprising over 3,000 standing stones erected between the 5th and 3rd millennia BC by Neolithic farming communities in Brittany. The alignments stretch across 15 kilometres, with the most complete group at Le Menec featuring eleven parallel rows of stones decreasing in size eastward, terminating in stone circles. Their purpose remains debated, with theories ranging from processional ways and sacred spaces to astronomical calendars marking solstice sunrise patterns. Scientific study began in the 19th century with Scotsman J. Miln, whose student Zacharie Le Rouzic continued excavations and ensured the stones'' preservation.\" -> \"The Carnac stones are an exceptionally dense collection of megalithic sites near the south coast of Brittany in northwestern France, consisting of stone alignments, dolmens, tumuli, and single menhirs [1]. Most of the stones are within the Breton municipality of Carnac, but some to the east are within neighboring La Trinité-sur-Mer [1]. The standing stones are made of weathered granite from local outcroppings that once extensively covered the area [1]. Three exceptionally large burial mounds are known from the Carnac and Morbihan area, dating from the mid-5th millennium BC and known collectively as ''Carnacéen tumuli'': Saint-Michel, Tumiac and Mané-er-Hroëk [1]. Each of these tumuli contained a megalithic burial chamber, containing the burial of only one individual, along with numerous large polished stone axeheads, stone arm-rings, and jewellery made from callaïs [1]. Scientific analyses have shown that many of the axeheads are made of jade from the Italian Alps, whilst the callaïs was imported from south-western Iberia [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159969", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-025: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/Mégalithes_vus_depuis_la_Maison_des_Mégalithes_à_Carnac_le_1er_septembre_2019_-_2.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315858", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/Mégalithes_vus_depuis_la_Maison_des_Mégalithes_à_Carnac_le_1er_septembre_2019_-_2.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b539ba9e/Carnac_megalith_alignment_1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b587a652-f222-47e6-adc9-b6ba1fa62722'::uuid, 'antiquity', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:b587a652-f222-47e6-adc9-b6ba1fa62722:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: antiquity ''5'' restored on Frankenbury Camp', 'fca43148881bccb8036779967f3012fe', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 6 from period_start -1500"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86952", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2157:chunk-0001: description \"Frankenbury Camp is an Iron Age univallate hillfort on the northwestern edge of the New Forest in Hampshire, and the largest Iron Age hillfort in the New Forest area. The fort encloses approximately 4.4 hectares (11 acres) on a slight promontory overlooking the Avon Valley, with very steep natural slopes defending the west and south sides, while the northeast is protected by a rampart and ditch. Classified by Historic England as a ''slight univallate hillfort'' dating from c. 700 BC to AD 43, such sites are relatively rare nationally with only around 150 examples recorded. It is a scheduled ancient monument (no. 122).\" -> ''Frankenbury Camp is the site of an Iron Age univallate hillfort located in Hampshire [1]. The site is on a very slight promontory overlooking the Avon Valley on the north-western edge of the New Forest [1]. The fort encloses approximately 11 acres [1]. It has very steep natural slopes on the west and south sides [1]. The northeast sides are defended by a simple rampart and ditch [1]. The original entrance on the southeast corner has since been widened [1].''"}, {"source