    ('bcefdb51-0003-46f4-806a-9bfca2b630e1'::uuid, 'mystery', '3', '2', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:bcefdb51-0003-46f4-806a-9bfca2b630e1:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''2'' restored on Glassonby', 'baef869e87cb4a105e56896190e58be8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 3 from (''City/town/settlement'', ''Undated'') is shared by 120 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37393", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0054:chunk-0001: description ''Glassonby stone circle, actually a kerbed cairn, lies 2 km north of the famous Long Meg and Her Daughters site in Cumbria. An oval arrangement of approximately 30 kerb stones (measuring 15.7 m by 14 m) sits atop a grass-covered cobble mound 30-33 m in diameter. Excavated by W.G. Collingwood in 1900, the site yielded a robbed cist, a cremation in a collared urn, burnt bones of a man, and a blue glass bead. Two stones bear carved concentric rings, spirals, and semi-ovoids, suggesting links to the wider Long Meg ritual complex.'' -> ''The Glassonby stone circle (actually a kerbed cairn) is at OS reference NY57293934 on private land [1]. An oval cairn is surrounded by a ring of kerb stones (30 stones in all, although some have been taken away and others added from field clearance over the years) [1]. Two of the stones had markings in the form of concentric rings or spirals and semi-ovoids [1]. The stones were not set in sockets, but were supported by the cairn material [1]. Outside the circle, burnt bones and an inverted collared urn were found [1].''"}, {