    ('c947e9b6-41c7-4c94-81c3-d79fc9e601b2'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:c947e9b6-41c7-4c94-81c3-d79fc9e601b2:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''8'' restored on Tambo Colorado', '60104fc978ca2c270d5fb29335179801', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Castle/palace'', ''1000 - 1500 AD'') is shared by 6 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:42044", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0058:chunk-0001: description \"Tambo Colorado is one of the best-preserved Inca adobe complexes on the Peruvian coast, located in the Pisco River Valley of the Ica Region. Built during the reign of Tupac Yupanqui in the late 15th century, the site features multiple structures arranged around a large trapezoidal central plaza up to 150 metres wide. Its adobe walls retain traces of the vivid horizontal stripes of red, black, white, and yellow ochre that once covered them, giving the site its name meaning ''red resting place''. The complex served as an administrative and military control center along the Inca Royal Road connecting the coast to the highlands, with characteristic recessed trapezoidal niches and a ceremonial ushnu platform.\" -> ''Tambo Colorado is a well-preserved Inca adobe complex near the coast of Peru, also known under the Quechua names Puka Tampu (\"red resting place\"), Pukallaqta (\"red place\") or Pukawasi (\"red house\") [1]. The site is located just inland from the south coast of Perú in the Pisco River Valley about 40 km along the highway to Ayacucho known as the Via de los Libertadores [1]. The site was most likely built at the end of the 15th century during the reign of the Inca king Pachacuti Inca Yupanqui also known as Pachacutec [1]. The site owes its name to the abundant use of colors on the walls [1]. Thanks to favorable environmental conditions, many walls at Tambo, both internal and external, retain enough residual colored paint to accurately reconstruct what the original wall painting would have been like [1]. The site consists of several structures around a large central plaza [1]. The central plaza is shaped like a trapezoid with its largest side being 150 m long [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162100", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-028: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/TamboColorado-colours.PNG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c947e9b6/Tambo Colorado, Peru.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334777", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/c947e9b6/Tambo Colorado, Peru.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c947e9b6/TamboColorado-colours.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
