of a tradition of stone circle construction that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age [1]. Positioned in a dry valley, the circle was erected on the southern end of a field that contained a range of natural sarsens [1]. Extensive flint knapping took place at the site during the Late Neolithic or Early Bronze Age [1]. The earliest known report of the site came from a Mr Falkner, who discovered it in 1840 while riding in the area [1]. The site was excavated in 2002 in an archaeological project led by Mark Gillings and Joshua Pollard [1]. Falkner''s Circle is 750m south-east of Avebury [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109748", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109749", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_start ''-2300'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254099", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254100", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353706", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353707", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:401885", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s004: period_name ''Undated'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:401886", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07c_mechanical-period-fix-s004: period_start None -> ''-3000''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('db227639-34b4-45db-817c-95092b4a916a'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:db227639-34b4-45db-817c-95092b4a916a:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Huaca Huallamarca', '3ec278c66b4dd6943aa7aad662d226b8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Temple complex'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 53 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29521", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0301:chunk-0001: period_start ''500'' -> ''-200''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30520", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:42172", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0062:chunk-0001: description ''Huaca Huallamarca, formerly called \"Pan de Azucar,\" is a truncated stepped adobe pyramid in the San Isidro district of Lima, Peru, originally measuring 125 meters long by 76 meters wide. Built around 200 BC by settlers of the Pinazo cultural tradition, it initially served as a ceremonial and administrative temple before transitioning to a cemetery around 300 AD. Excavations in 1958 by Arturo Jimenez Borja uncovered 48 funerary bundles from the Lima, Huaura, Sican, Chincha, and Ychsma cultures. The site museum, inaugurated in 1960, was one of Peru\\''s first site museums.'' -> ''Huaca Huallamarca (possibly from Quechua wak\\''a, Spanishified as \"huaca\" and meaning \"sacred\" or \"local god of protection\"; Walla, the name of the Huallas tribe; and marka, \"village\", \"town\" or \"region\"), also formerly known as Huaca Pan de Azúcar, is an archaeological site located in the district of San Isidro, in the city of Lima [1]. It has the form of a truncated stepped pyramid [1]. The first archaeological excavations were carried out in 1942 and were directed by Julio C. Tello, who stated that Huaca Huallamarca\\''s boundaries were marked by the Huatica and Surco canals [1]. Tello also identified three main moments of occupation: from the beginning of the Lima culture period (2nd century BCE to 7th century AD), through the Yshma (11th century AD), to the Inca period (15th–16th centuries AD) [1]. The construction of the Huallamarca pyramid began with the Huallas from 200 BC to AD 200 [1]. Archaeologist Julio C. Tello described Huallamarca as a structure built exclusively with small handmade adobes in the shape of molars or irregular cones [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163826", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/db227639/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/db227639/Huaca - Huallamarca.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318390", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/db227639/Huaca - Huallamarca.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/db227639/San_Isidro_Huallamarca_view01.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
