ine [1]. It is 1.3 metres wide and 1.1 metres high; the circular hole is 0.5 m in diameter [1]. The Mên-an-Tol is thought to date to either the late Neolithic or early Bronze Age [1]. In 1749 the site was first archaeologically investigated by William Borlase, who also drew a plan [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110515", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110516", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164473", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e14380ae/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e14380ae/Men_An_Tol_Cornwall_by_Kernow_Skies.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318492", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e14380ae/Men_An_Tol_Cornwall_by_Kernow_Skies.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e14380ae/Menantol5.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
