    ('e34f699d-7025-4f41-ab0c-fdb54d6a672c'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:e34f699d-7025-4f41-ab0c-fdb54d6a672c:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Tholos of Delphi', 'e19b80ee28dedbc84d7f5d18bfd3e88a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Temple complex'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 53 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88086", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2190:chunk-0001: description \"The Tholos of Delphi is a circular temple within the Sanctuary of Athena Pronaia, designed by architect Theodore the Phocaean between 380-370 BC. The structure featured 20 Doric columns on the exterior colonnade and 10 or 13 Corinthian semi-columns inside the cella, with metopes depicting Amazonomachy and Centauromachy in high relief. Its floor combined white marble at the center with dark limestone from Eleusis toward the circumference. The building''s function remains uncertain, with theories ranging from an arsenal to a sanctuary of Artemis. It is part of the Delphi UNESCO World Heritage Site.\" -> ''The Tholos of Delphi is among the ancient structures of the Sanctuary of Athena Pronaia in Delphi [1]. The circular temple, a tholos, shares the immediate site with other ancient foundations of the Temple of Athena Pronaia, all located less than a mile east of the main ruins at Delphi [1]. The architect of the \"vaulted temple at Delphi\" is named by Vitruvius in De architectura Book VII as Theodorus Phoceus (not Theodorus of Samos, whom Vitruvius names separately) [1]. Externally, twenty Doric columns supported a frieze with triglyphs and metopes [1]. Materials used included Eleusinian thin slabs and Pentelic marble in the superstructure and limestone at the platform [1]. In the major metopes of the outer side there are scene representations from Amazon and Centaur battles [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164525", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/Tholos_%2826307490985%29.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e34f699d/Tholos_(26307490985).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
