 carried out in November 1859, under antiquarian Augustus Wollaston Franks [1]. His workers cleared out an Iron Age pit that was 2 metres deep and 1.5 m in diameter, although they were not able to ascertain its purpose [1]. The hillfort was occupied from the mid-6th century BC until around 100 BC, and the defences were remodelled numerous times [1]. The north part of the fort was occupied by four-post structures, probably granaries, which were later replaced by storage pits, and in the south part, there were roundhouses in between granaries and storage pits [1]. The Danebury meteorite is an H5 ordinary chondrite meteorite found in 1974 during the excavation of a grain pit at the hillfort [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:165068", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-032: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e5d1f9a5/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e5d1f9a5/Danebury Iron Age Hill Fort (4490430016).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318589", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/e5d1f9a5/Danebury Iron Age Hill Fort (4490430016).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/e5d1f9a5/Danebury_Fort_-_aerial_image,_Hampshire_Data_Portal.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
