    ('f80f56c0-949f-4009-8658-f47cc3ca13fa'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:f80f56c0-949f-4009-8658-f47cc3ca13fa:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''5'' restored on Butkara Stupa', 'be9a6e847c427a412453d5cba2f187ad', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Temple complex'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 53 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43239", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0069:chunk-0001: description ''The Butkara Stupa is a significant Buddhist monument near Mingora in Swat Valley, Pakistan. Originally possibly commissioned by Emperor Ashoka, it is now generally dated to the 2nd century BCE [1]. Archaeological excavations have yielded numerous artifacts including coins and Kharosthi inscriptions, with discoveries dating to around 2,000 years ago [2].'' -> ''The Butkara Stupa is an important Buddhist stupa near Mingora, in the area of Swat [1]. The stupa was excavated by an Italian mission, led by archaeologist Domenico Faccenna from 1956 [1]. The mission established that the stupa was \"monumentalized\" by the addition of Hellenistic architectural decorations during the 2nd century BCE, suggesting a direct involvement of the Indo-Greeks, rulers of Indus during that period, in the development of Greco-Buddhist architecture [1]. An Indo-Corinthian capital representing a Buddhist devotee within foliage has been found which had a reliquary and a coin of Azes II buried at its base, securely dating the sculpture to earlier than 20 BCE [1]. A large quantity of the artifacts are preserved in the National Museum of Oriental Art and the City Museum of Ancient Oriental Art in Turin [1]. The in-situ seated Buddha (or Bodhisattva) statue at Butkara is considered one of the earliest, if not the earliest, known iconographical statues of the Buddha in northwestern India [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:166641", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-034: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f80f56c0/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f80f56c0/Butkara 1 front view.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:319982", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f80f56c0/Butkara 1 front view.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f80f56c0/General_View_of_the_main_Butkara-I_Stupa.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
