    ('f9bb5abb-8ef2-4a21-adb5-2504fc9e87bb'::uuid, 'mystery', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-07c-rollback:f9bb5abb-8ef2-4a21-adb5-2504fc9e87bb:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07c: mystery ''4'' restored on Chanctonbury Ring', 'ed55864a5f20f6b329d55f5718aa5cf5', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 1 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 235 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export 80b2b77ea294dfe0 (2026-11-07 13:29:26.934212+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 766 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07''s own export (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28645", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0174:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-700''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30545", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43245", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0069:chunk-0001: description ''Chanctonbury Ring is a prehistoric hillfort situated atop Chanctonbury Hill on the South Downs in West Sussex, England[1]. The site occupies a strategic position on the Sussex Downs, with a ridgeway—now part of the South Downs Way—running along the hill[1]. It forms part of a complex of associated historical features spanning over 2,000 years, including Bronze Age to Saxon round barrows and Iron Age to Roman period dykes[1]. The hillfort represents a significant prehistoric defensive structure in southern England.'' -> ''Chanctonbury Ring is a prehistoric hill fort atop Chanctonbury Hill on the South Downs, on the border of the civil parishes of Washington and Wiston in the English county of West Sussex [1]. Consisting of a roughly circular low earthen rampart surrounded by a ditch, Chanctonbury Ring is thought to date to the late Bronze Age or early Iron Age [1]. The hill fort is roughly circular, enclosing about 1.5 hectares (3.7 acres) [1]. Chanctonbury Ring was investigated by archeologists in excavations carried out in 1869, 1909, 1977 and 1988–91 [1]. Evidence for human activity in the Neolithic and early Bronze Age has been found, but not settlement or occupation [1]. Around five hundred years after its initial abandonment, Chanctonbury Ring was reused as a religious site from the mid-first century AD [1]. At least two Romano-Celtic temples were built in the interior, probably some time during the second century, while the bank and ditch were reused to form a temenos or \"sacred precinct\" [1]. Mosaic fragments from Chanctonbury Ring can be found at the Horsham Museum [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:166762", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-034: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f9bb5abb/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f9bb5abb/Chanctonbury_Ring_bank_south-west_view.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320009", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f9bb5abb/Chanctonbury_Ring_bank_south-west_view.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f9bb5abb/Chanctonbury_Ring_south-east_view.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
